S[1]=dict(lbl="What is playful work design?|Who wrote this chapter, and why stay critical?|What problem does it solve?|How does play at work help?|What are the two kinds of play?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("What is playful work design, and when do you need it?",
 "Reading 5 showed how employees change their job. This last reading shows a lighter tool: changing how you do the same task. It answers the theme question for the hardest days, the ones full of hassles or the same task again and again.",
 ["What playful work design is","Who wrote the chapter, and why that matters","The problem it solves","The two kinds of play behind it"]),
CARD("Making your own work activities more fun or more challenging, on your own initiative, without necessarily changing the job itself.",
 "The formal definition: \"the proactive cognitive-behavioral orientation aimed at fostering fun and challenge during work activities through creating, seeking, and resolving surprises and complexities\" (Scharp et al., 2023, p. 7). <b>Proactive</b> means on your own initiative; <b>cognitive-behavioral</b> means it changes both how you think about the task and what you do. Unlike job crafting, which changes the job's demands and resources, <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"pwd\">playful work design</b> works at the level of the <b>task</b>: you approach the same work differently.",
 "an exam option calling playful work design a form of job crafting that changes demands and resources is wrong.",
 "who wrote it."),
CARD("Arnold Bakker and Yuri Scharp, who did much of the research on the concept themselves: a strength, and a reason to stay critical.",
 "Bakker is at Erasmus University Rotterdam and the University of Johannesburg, Scharp at Tilburg University. Scharp et al. (2023), with Bakker, developed the questionnaire that measures playful work design. The chapter was written for Parker et al. (2024). It reviews the \"emerging literature\": predictors, outcomes, boundaries, practice and future research. Several studies it cites are the authors' own, and several are unpublished or still under review.",
 "the people who built the field know it best, but they also review their own work.",
 "the problem it solves."),
CARD("How employees stay engaged on days with hindrance demands or monotonous work.",
 "Job design research shows people can thrive in challenging, resourceful jobs. But some days are full of hassles, conflict, or the same task again and again. <b>Hindrance demands</b> are parts of work that only get in your way, such as bureaucratic hassles and interpersonal conflicts (LePine et al., 2005). The authors' answer is a new bottom-up approach that works on the task itself. <i>Our example:</i> a data-entry clerk facing 300 identical forms.",
 "playful work design is a tool for the days the job itself cannot easily change.",
 "what play does at work."),
CARD("Through better relationships, more task involvement and better thinking, and less stress and boredom; research shows mostly favourable effects, though play can sometimes clash with work.",
 "Play is an activity done for amusement and fun, with an enthusiastic, in-the-moment attitude, and highly interactive with partners or the activity (Van Vleet &amp; Feeney, 2015). At work it improves relationships with clients and colleagues, task involvement, divergent thinking and problem solving, and reduces stress and boredom, with more learning and creativity. <b>Divergent thinking</b> means coming up with many different ideas. Play may at times be at odds with work and have social costs (Petelczyc et al., 2018).",
 "play at work is not a distraction by default; done well, it supports the work.",
 "the two kinds."),
CARD("Ludic play, spontaneous fun, and agonistic play, serious contest (Huizinga).",
 "In his book <i>Homo Ludens</i> (1949), Dutch philosopher Johan Huizinga distinguished <b>ludic play</b> (humour, fantasy, jokes, imagination) and <b>agonistic play</b> (challenges, rules and goals, like sports and games). <i>Ludic</i> comes from the Latin <i>ludus</i> (games and jokes) and is spontaneous and irrational; <i>agonistic</i> comes from the Greek <i>agōn</i> (contest) and is serious and rational. They become the two strategies: <b>designing fun</b> and <b>designing competition</b>. <i>Our example:</i> washing the dishes, you can sing while you do it (ludic) or try to finish before the song ends (agonistic).",
 "fun and competition are the two building blocks of everything that follows.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("Playful work design makes existing tasks more fun or challenging on your own initiative, to stay engaged on hard or dull days.",
 [("What is it?","Proactively fostering fun and challenge in work activities, at task level."),("Who wrote it?","Bakker and Scharp, who built the field; stay critical."),("Problem?","Staying engaged with hindrances or monotony."),("Play at work?","Mostly favourable effects."),("Two kinds?","Ludic (fun) and agonistic (competition).")],
 ["What is the difference between ludic and agonistic play?","Which statement is wrong? \"Playful work design changes the job's demands and resources.\""])])

